      "message": "Automated review (Claude Sonnet Latest)\nReviewed by: Claude Sonnet Latest (openrouter/~anthropic/claude-sonnet-latest)\n\n# Review of Change 1015910, patch set 1 — `feat(designate-bind-k8s): add forwarders option`\n\nRevision: `5bdab89c056feb541736aa587b42f00c6b8217f1` (openstack/sunbeam-charms, branch `main`)\nFiles: `charms/designate-bind-k8s/charmcraft.yaml`, `.../src/templates/named.conf.options.j2`, `.../tests/unit/test_bind_scenario.py`\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change adds two `string` config options (`forwarders`, `allowed_recursion_nets`) to the\ndesignate-bind-k8s charm and renders them in `named.conf.options.j2` as a `forwarders { ... };`\nblock plus `forward only;`, `recursion yes;` and an `allow-recursion` block. The Jinja\nwhitespace control (`{%-`) is used consistently and the rendered output I traced by hand is\nsyntactically valid BIND9 in all three states (forwarders unset / forwarders set / forwarders +\nnets set): the stripped newlines land the block at the same 8-space indentation as the\nsurrounding `options` statements, and no stray blank line is left inside the block. The commit\nmessage follows repo convention (conventional-commit subject, `Change-Id`, `Signed-off-by`,\n`Closes-Bug: #2171896`) and the file-filter wiring is correct: `zuul.d/jobs.yaml:124` and\n`zuul.d/jobs.yaml:494` both list `charms/designate-bind-k8s/`, so the charm\u0027s build and the\nrepo-wide unit/pep8/linters jobs are triggered by this path.\n\nThe substantive problem is the security default: when `forwarders` is set and\n`allowed_recursion_nets` is not, the template now emits `allow-recursion { any; };`. Before this\nchange the template set neither `recursion` nor `allow-recursion`, so BIND9\u0027s own defaults\n(`allow-recursion` falls back to `allow-query-cache`, i.e. `{ localnets; localhost; }`) applied\nand recursion was *not* open. Because this charm publishes DNS on port 53 through a Kubernetes\nLoadBalancer (`charm.py` `default_public_ingress_port \u003d 53`, `allow-query { any; }`), the new\nexplicit `any` turns the unit into an internet-reachable open resolver. There is also no\nvalidation of the operator-supplied strings before they are written into `named.conf`, so a\nmalformed value breaks `named.conf` rather than failing fast.\n\n## CI / Zuul results consulted\n\nRead anonymously from Gerrit (`/changes/1015910/detail?o\u003dLABELS\u0026o\u003dMESSAGES`), latest check run\n(buildset `cf6e4dafb8734e6baf718544cf25c82d`, `Verified +1` by Zuul at 2026-11-17 02:15 UTC):\n\n- `openstack-tox-linters` — SUCCESS (3m 40s)\n- `openstack-tox-pep8` — SUCCESS (1m 49s)\n- `openstack-tox-py314` — SUCCESS (6m 28s)\n- `openstack-tox-cover` — SUCCESS (13m 50s)\n- `charm-build-bin-services-a` — SUCCESS (10m 01s)\n- `func-test-openstack-microovn` — SUCCESS (1h 09m 30s)\n\nAn earlier run had `Verified -1` with `func-test-openstack-microovn` FAILURE; the author replied\nthat it was an unrelated node stall (`/PATCHSET_LEVEL` comment, 2026-11-17 00:54) and the recheck\npassed. I did not run any linters, tests or builds myself.\n\n## Findings\n\n### Critical: 0\n\nNone.\n\n### High: 1\n\n**H1. `allow-recursion { any; };` opens recursion to every host — a regression versus the previous BIND default.**\n`charms/designate-bind-k8s/src/templates/named.conf.options.j2:41` (with the gate at\n`:30-34` and the doc at `charms/designate-bind-k8s/charmcraft.yaml:66-68`).\n\nWhat is wrong: when `forwarders` is non-empty and `allowed_recursion_nets` is empty, the template\nemits `allow-recursion { any; };`. Why it matters: `HEAD~1`\u0027s template had no `recursion` and no\n`allow-recursion`, so BIND9 defaulted `allow-recursion` to `allow-query-cache`\n(`{ localnets; localhost; }`); `allow-query { any; }` alone does not open recursion. The new\nline is therefore a widening of the recursion policy, not a neutral default, and it is combined\nwith `forward only`, so any host that can reach the LoadBalancer (the charm declares\n`default_public_ingress_port \u003d 53` and opens UDP/TCP 53) gets an open recursive resolver that\nwill forward arbitrary queries upstream — the classic DNS amplification/reflection and cache-\npoisoning exposure. The commit message justifies this as \"matching the machine charm\", but a\nmachine charm\u0027s local-network default is not equivalent to a K8s charm fronted by a public\n`LoadBalancer`; that parity claim is also not verifiable from this worktree (there is no\n`designate-bind` machine charm here). Suggested fix: do not render `any` by default. Either make\n`allowed_recursion_nets` effectively required when `forwarders` is set (fail the unit into\n`BlockedStatus` with a clear message if it is unset), or default the rendered value to a safe\nset (e.g. the cluster/pod CIDRs, or BIND\u0027s `localnets`) instead of `any`, and keep the current\n\"allow any\" behaviour only behind an explicit opt-in. At minimum, the charm should not report\n`ActiveStatus` while it is an open resolver.\n\n### Medium: 2\n\n**M1. Operator-supplied config is interpolated into `named.conf` without per-entry validation (config injection / broken config).**\n`charms/designate-bind-k8s/src/templates/named.conf.options.j2:11-12` and `:36-37`; option\ndefinitions at `charms/designate-bind-k8s/charmcraft.yaml:43-68`.\n\nWhat is wrong: each entry is only `strip()`-ed and then written verbatim as `{{ entry }};`.\n`split(\u0027;\u0027)` prevents a literal `;` in an entry, but nothing prevents newlines, `{`/`}`, `#` or\n`//` inside an entry. A value such as `forwarders: \"8.8.8.8\\n        }\"` renders\n`8.8.8.8\\n        };` — the entry\u0027s `}` plus the auto-appended `;` closes the block early — and a\nvalue containing a newline can add arbitrary text lines to the `options` block. There is also no\ncheck that a forwarder is an IP address (BIND requires addresses, not hostnames) or that a\nrecursion net is a valid CIDR. Why it matters: the failure mode is a syntactically invalid\n`named.conf`, which makes `named` fail to start (the rock plan runs `named-checkconf` as a Pebble\ncheck) and takes the DNS backend down instead of rejecting the bad value at config time; the\ninput is operator-supplied via `juju config`, so this is a robustness/DoS issue rather than a\nprivilege boundary. Suggested fix: validate in `charm.py`\u0027s config-changed path (or before\nrendering) using `ipaddress.ip_address` for each `forwarders` entry and\n`ipaddress.ip_network(..., strict\u003dFalse)` for each net, and put the unit in `BlockedStatus` on\nfailure. Additionally reject entries containing whitespace other than the surrounding padding,\nand drop the `-`/`strip()`-only approach in favour of pre-validated lists passed to the template.\n\n**M2. The new tests do not assert rendered BIND syntax or indentation, and one of them enshrines the insecure default.**\n`charms/designate-bind-k8s/tests/unit/test_bind_scenario.py:107-121` (and `:97-105`, `:123-137`).\n\nWhat is wrong: the tests only assert substring presence/ordering and a semicolon-split list of\nentries; they never assert the exact rendered text, indentation, or that the result is\nparseable. `test_forwarders_rendered` explicitly asserts\n`assert \"allow-recursion { any; };\" in content` (line 121), so the open-resolver default becomes\na locked-in expectation rather than something a future fix can change freely. The helper\n`_block_entries` (line 94) would also mask indentation regressions, since it strips each entry.\nWhy it matters: the whole risk of this change is in the rendered text (whitespace control inside\na `named.conf`), which is exactly what is not asserted; a broken render would pass these tests as\nlong as the substrings appear. Suggested fix: assert the full expected rendered\n`named.conf.options` (or at least the exact block text with indentation) for the three states,\nadd a case with a newline/`}` in `forwarders` to pin the validation behaviour, and change the\n\"any\" assertion once H1 is addressed. A `named-checkconf`-based assertion (or invoking it against\nthe rendered file in the unit test) would give real syntax coverage.\n\n### Low: 3\n\n**L1. A whitespace-only `forwarders` value renders an empty block plus `forward only;`.**\n`charms/designate-bind-k8s/src/templates/named.conf.options.j2:9-15`. `if options.forwarders` is\ntruthy for `\" \"`/`\";;;\"`, while the `for ... if forwarder.strip()` filter drops every entry, so\nthe file gets `forwarders { };` followed by `forward only;`. BIND accepts an empty forwarders\nlist syntactically, but `forward only` with no usable forwarder silently breaks resolution for\nall non-authoritative names. Suggest treating a value whose stripped entries are all empty as\n\"unset\" (validate/normalise in the charm) so the config stays unchanged.\n\n**L2. `allowed_recursion_nets` is silently ignored when `forwarders` is unset.**\n`charms/designate-bind-k8s/src/templates/named.conf.options.j2:34`; documented at\n`charms/designate-bind-k8s/charmcraft.yaml:66-67`; asserted at\n`tests/unit/test_bind_scenario.py:97-105`. The doc says the option \"only takes effect when the\nforwarders option is set\", but the operator gets no feedback that the value they set is inert.\nSuggest logging a warning (or returning `BlockedStatus`/`WaitingStatus`) when\n`allowed_recursion_nets` is set without `forwarders`.\n\n**L3. Parity with the machine charm is claimed but not demonstrable here, and the option naming is only locally consistent.**\n`charms/designate-bind-k8s/charmcraft.yaml:54-57` and `:66-68`; commit message. The\n\"matching the machine charm\" statement cannot be checked from this worktree (no `designate-bind`\nmachine charm source is present), and for the K8s charm the security context differs (public\n`LoadBalancer` on 53). `allowed_recursion_nets` uses an underscore, which is consistent with the\nneighbouring `loadbalancer_annotations`, but if the machine charm exposes a differently named\noption (`allow-recursion`/`allowed_recursion_nets`) the two charms will diverge in operator UX.\nSuggest confirming the exact upstream option names/behaviour and, if parity is a goal, citing\nthe machine charm commit in the message; otherwise drop the parity claim and document the K8s\ndefault explicitly.\n\n### Nit: 2\n\n**N1.** `charms/designate-bind-k8s/src/templates/named.conf.options.j2:8` leaves the comment\n`// Forwarders are managed by the charm \"forwarders\" config option.` in place even when\n`forwarders` is unset, where it describes a feature that is not active. Minor; could be moved\ninside the `{%- if %}`.\n\n**N2.** `tests/unit/test_bind_scenario.py:108` docstring says \"Forwarders are sanitised\", but the\ncode performs no sanitisation beyond `strip()` (see M1). Either implement the sanitisation the\ndocstring promises or reword it. For completeness: the new `import dataclasses`\n(`test_bind_scenario.py:19`) is genuinely used by `dataclasses.replace` at line 83, so it is not\na dead import, and `git status --porcelain` in this worktree was empty when reviewed.\n\n## Suggested vote\n\nSuggested vote: Code-Review -1\n\nThe change is functionally correct, well-tested at the substring level, and its Zuul check\npipeline is green, but H1 introduces an internet-reachable open resolver default and M1 lets\nmalformed operator input break `named.conf`. I would re-vote +1 once the recursion default is\nmade safe (or `allowed_recursion_nets` is required when `forwarders` is set) and the config\nvalues are validated.\n\nauto-review:5bdab89c056feb541736aa587b42f00c6b8217f1",
